# Body review: arch-body.stl SHA256 42f3aa6e4ffe1844bebbe7358e7c75b799f8ac9e95b8e293a257ffa2de185b1a.
# All six classes were read once from the published geometry. Thin and ledge remain rankings.
# These explanations establish geometry and print intent; physical release, cleanup, finish, strength and lifetime are unmeasured.

[step] The central slide plinth ends at Z3.08, while the ordinary enclosure floor ends at Z3.00. The 0.08 mm local step places the plinth on the common first-layer 0.20 plus twelve 0.24 mm layer datum; the moving crossbar starts at Z3.56, preserving its single 0.48 mm lower release gap. The exposed plinth top is a functional running reference, not a second calibration variation.
alternatives: Lowering only the plinth to Z3.00 changes the release gap. Raising the entire ordinary floor to Z3.08 adds material outside the mechanism without improving that datum. Translating the complete moving/capture stack changes its layer phase and requires a different native release reading.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=0.000 y=14.750 z=3.080

[sliver] These four 1.2 mm faces bound the local flute backing pad. The pad is joined over its full area to the ordinary 3 mm wall; none of its 1.2 mm edge widths is a freestanding wall thickness. The additional backing accommodates the source's 1.2 mm deep flutes while retaining 3 mm behind the deepest groove.
alternatives: Thickening the entire flank to 4.2 mm gives the same flute reading with additional material outside the four-flute field. Removing the backing leaves only 1.8 mm behind a full-depth groove. Extending the pad to the bed removes its lower edge but adds stock outside the useful texture field.
