[sliver] Each 0.25 mm strip is the visible fore-face at an end of the rear guide tie: the tie ends at X18.25 in magnitude and the adjoining stop ends at X18.00. The guide's material thickness is 3 mm along Y, from Y24.5 to Y27.5, and it is joined to the stop and connected floor. The narrow exposed face is not a 0.25 mm wall or free fin.
alternatives: Extending the stop outboard to the tie end hides this face but adds stock without changing its 3 mm normal thickness or the working stop plane. Increasing the entire rear guide to 4 mm along Y adds material and reduces interior access without changing the slide clearance.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=-18.125 y=24.500 z=6.700
click: x=18.125 y=24.500 z=6.700

[ceiling] The flat Z38 underside is the 40 by 3 mm header of the rear support-removal opening. Automatic supports are retained for this accessible fixed underside; the 40 by 32 mm opening below it exposes the route to the inner crown and its supports. Removal effort remains a physical print result.
alternatives: A 45 degree corbel replaces this flat header and narrows the access opening. Lowering the header reduces access to the crown's support mat. Adding roof thickness above the header does not improve deposition on its first underside layer.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=0.000 y=46.500 z=38.000

[ceiling] This 20 by 1.2 mm underside is the local flute backing pad's short inward projection. Its adjoining 3 mm wall roots it continuously. Automatic supports are permitted here and the inner face is reachable through the rear access opening; support removal is not physically qualified.
alternatives: Continuing the backing to the bed adds 0.432 cubic centimetres outside the texture field. A ramp changes the inner boundary and adds material. Thickening the entire flank removes the local ledge at greater material cost without adding a useful flute reading.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=26.400 y=20.000 z=18.000

[ceiling] These are the two fixed capture-lip undersides at Z7.40. The body-only lint omits the moving shoulders beneath them, whose tops are Z6.92, so the designed model-to-model air is 0.48 mm rather than the reported 4.3 mm drop to the fixed plinth. Each lip is rooted to its 3 mm guide wall. The native project distinguishes this release air from removable ceiling-support roots in the empty terminal travel pockets; it does not promise that every swept pocket is free of supports. Release and cleanup remain physical results.
alternatives: A corbel below a lip consumes the intended release air or moving stroke. Increasing lip thickness above its 3 mm stock adds material without improving its first-layer release. A larger gap is a candidate for a separate whole-object print and changes the fit being measured.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=0.000 y=8.125 z=7.400
click: x=0.000 y=21.375 z=7.400

[thin] These two short diagonals occur only where the retained curved R6 junction ends at the planar fore-guide end. The indicated surface regions span about 1 by 0.9 by 1.9 mm. Material traced inward along the curved-face normal continues for 5.982 mm and 5.921 mm; inward from the guide-end face it continues into broad connected stock. The 1.046 and 1.119 mm diagonals therefore describe a finite curve/end-face corner, not the section of a thin stationary blade or the guide's load path. The ordinary guide normal thickness remains 3 mm. This scope does not qualify the corner's printed finish.
alternatives: Extending the guide outboard or flattening the R6 junction adds stock and obscures the retained bed/round intersection without increasing the guide's principal 3 mm section. Cropping or chamfering the finite corner changes that local reference junction rather than correcting a thin plate.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
