[thin] This 3.000 mm reading is the bed-rooted fore-guide wall between Y2 and Y5. It supports the fixed front capture lip and defines the outer side of the moving-leg corridor. Its working face and gap are fixed in this whole-object variation.
alternatives: Thickening toward the moving leg consumes its 0.25 mm per-face gap. Thickening outboard to a 4 mm wall adds stock to the front aperture without improving the first lip layer. Moving the complete guide and counterpart to restore the gap changes the compact mechanism's allocation.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
click: x=-13.812 y=2.000 z=0.899
click: x=-13.812 y=5.000 z=0.899

[thin] These two small 3.000 mm readings are the rear-guide tie's normal stock between Y24.5 and Y27.5. They occur beside the exposed 0.25 mm end strips, whose face width does not reduce this wall thickness. The tie is rooted on the connected floor and adjoins the fixed stop.
alternatives: Making the rear guide 4 mm thick inward changes the moving-leg gap; making it 4 mm outward adds material and reduces support-removal space. Extending the outboard stops to hide the end strips does not increase the tie's normal stock.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-body.step
